<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Conservative Judaism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Masorti Judaism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Conservative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, also known as Masorti Judaism, is a Jewish religious movement that holds Jewish law, or Halakha, to be both binding and subject to historical development, its authority resting chiefly on the assent of the people through the generations. It regards itself as heir to the positive-historical school of Rabbi Zecharias Frankel in 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, but became fully institutionalised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the mid-20th century. The United Synagogue was founded in 1913, and the Jewish Theological Seminary of America is its largest seminary. In 1983 it resolved to ordain women rabbis. It is the third-largest Jewish movement, close to 1.1 million people.</p></div>
